# Forbidden patterns for Go files (*.go). Applied by .githooks/pre-commit.
# scaffold-extensions: go
# Format: <regex><TAB><description>   (one per line; # comments ignored)
# Separator is TAB - patterns can contain literal `|` for ERE alternation.
# Case-sensitive ERE (grep -anE). Copy to your project as `.forbidden-patterns/go.txt`.
# Active rules flag leftover debug output plus two security sinks (TLS bypass,
# a dynamic shell command); panic/log.Fatal live in optional below.

(^|[^A-Za-z_.])fmt\.Print(ln|f)?[[:space:]]*\(	Remove fmt.Print/Println/Printf debug output - use the project logger
(^|[^A-Za-z_.])println[[:space:]]*\(	Remove the builtin println() before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_.])spew\.Dump[[:space:]]*\(	Remove spew.Dump() debug dump before committing

# --- Security: TLS bypass / command injection ---
# `go vet` catches neither and the golangci-lint CI job ships commented out, so
# nothing else in the scaffold sees these.
#
# TLS: the Go twin of backend.txt's `verify=False` and frontend.txt's
# `rejectUnauthorized: false`, and it inherits their one known cost - a test
# dialing a self-signed httptest server trips it. That is a true positive the
# team may choose to accept, so mark those lines `// scaffold-allow`; most can
# instead drop the field and use httptest.NewTLSServer(h).Client(). Only `: true`
# fires: `InsecureSkipVerify: false`, `c.InsecureSkipVerify = insecure`, `if
# c.InsecureSkipVerify {` and prose mentioning the field stay silent (144 lines
# name the field across two Go distributions; 40 match, all of them real bypasses).
#
# exec: deliberately narrow - it needs ALL THREE of a literal shell as argv[0]
# (sh/bash/zsh/ksh/dash, optionally /bin/- or /usr/bin/-prefixed, in the
# exec.Command("sh", ...) or exec.CommandContext(ctx, "sh", ...) form), a `-c`
# flag, and a script argument that is NOT one plain string literal. The third
# clause is what keeps it honest: exec.Command("bash", "-c", "set -e; make") is a
# fixed script with nothing to interpolate into and does NOT fire, while
# exec.Command("sh", "-c", userInput), fmt.Sprintf(...) and "echo "+name do.
# Also silent: a shell in a later argument (exec.Command("which", "bash"),
# exec.Command("docker", "run", "alpine", "sh")), a variable program path
# (exec.Command(binPath, "-c", script)), a shell running a script FILE
# (exec.Command("bash", "./deploy.sh")), and a bare exec.Command("sh").
# Over the 21,008 .go files of two Go distributions it matched 2 lines, both the
# fmt.Sprintf-built-shell-script shape, and nothing else.
InsecureSkipVerify[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*true	InsecureSkipVerify: true disables TLS cert validation (MITM risk) - fix the cert/CA, don't disable verification
(^|[^A-Za-z_.])exec\.(Command\(|CommandContext\([^,]*,)[[:space:]]*"(/(usr/)?bin/)?(sh|bash|zsh|ksh|dash)"[[:space:]]*,[[:space:]]*"-[a-z]*c"[[:space:]]*,[[:space:]]*("[^"]*"[[:space:]]*\+|[^"[:space:]])	Shell -c with a built-up script string is a command-injection sink - pass the program and its args to exec.Command directly

# Optional (opt-in) — uncomment a line to enable it:
# (^|[^A-Za-z_.])print[[:space:]]*\(	Remove the builtin print() before committing (off by default: 'print' is a plausible method/field name)
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])panic[[:space:]]*\(	Avoid panic() in library code - return an error (off by default: legitimate in main/init and tests)
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])log\.(Fatal|Panic)(f|ln)?[[:space:]]*\(	Avoid log.Fatal/log.Panic in library code - return an error (off by default: legitimate in main)

# No ignored-error rule ships here, not even commented out. Go's `_` discards
# ordinary values as well as errors, and a line-oriented regex cannot tell
# `r0, _, _ := syscall.Syscall(...)`, `req, _ := http.NewRequest(...)` or the
# package-level `var _ = flag.Bool(...)` from a dropped error. The obvious
# pattern matched 11,217 lines in 2,694 of the 21,008 .go files of two Go
# distributions - 13% of every file - so even as an opt-in it is a trap. Use
# errcheck / golangci-lint, which decide this on the AST.
